't propose recreating these packages. Now I need to check what the use cases actually require, particularly around test coverage, since the source_code doesn't show any test files yet. Looking at the priority rules, testutils is the most foundational since it's the testing harness everything else depends on, so proposing tests for uc004 makes the most sense given the constraint of only one task.\n\nNow I'm examining the requirement references across the codebase—testutils has extensive coverage across multiple requirement sections, while the format package shows partial references that appear to be cut off.\n\nLooking at the dependency structure, testutils serves as the differential testing harness for command-line utilities, whereas the pkg-level packages like format and sys use standard Go testing, which changes how I should prioritize them.\n\nGiven the constraint of proposing just one task with a maximum of 250 lines and 4 requirements, I'll go with the first option alphabetically: writing tests for pkg/format/. The format package includes functions for color management and output formatting, so I'd need to cover the color control functions and the various formatting utilities it provides. I'm seeing that the requirements span across multiple functions, so I need to respect the constraint that each task covers at most 4 requirements. Let me focus on the table and alignment functions from R1 since those form a cohesive unit for testing. I'm realizing the pipeline here—the stitch agent generates the code, so tests aren't actually blocking that work. Since I need to pick one task and follow alphabetical order per the guidelines, I'll focus on the table tests covering the Columns, PadRight, and PadLeft functions. Let me figure out what test files I need to create and keep it within the 4-requirement, 250-line limit. I'm mapping out the test file structure for table alignment functions. I have four distinct test requirements covering column distribution, padding functions, display width calculation for UTF-8 strings, and row-major ordering. This should be straightforward to implement in under 250 lines. I need to write unit tests covering the padding functions and column distribution logic, ensuring they handle edge cases like empty input and multi-byte UTF-8 strings correctly, while using table-driven tests to access the unexported displayWidth function.\n\nNow I'm checking the PRD file naming convention to make sure I'm referencing the right documentation structure. prd002-sys, prd003-format\n\nThe source code references \"prd003-format\" so the file should be docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml.\n\nWait, but I also see references to \"prd001-testutils\", \"prd002-sys\". Let me check the docs path pattern. Looking at project_structure:\n- docs/specs/product-requirements/ - Per-utility PRDs; also PRDs for shared pkg/ components\n\nAnd from the use case file naming: docs/specs/use-cases/rel[NN].[N]-uc[NNN]-[short-name].yaml\n\nSo for rel00.0-uc002-format, the use case file would be at docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc002-format.yaml.\n\nTest suites live in docs/specs/test-suites/ with naming like test-rel-[release-id].yaml, so I'd expect docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-00.0.yaml.\n\nNow let me verify the scope constraint. I can only propose tasks for rel00.0 use cases, and checking the list: uc001-magefiles is excluded since it's in the magefiles/ path, but uc002-format, uc003-sys, and uc004-testutils all have existing code that needs tests. Following alphabetical ordering per P8, that's format, sys, then testutils.\n\nI'm checking my task requirements against the constraints: max 4 requirements per task (I have exactly 4), target 150-250 lines per test file (a table-driven test suite for 4 functions should land around 150-200 lines). I should also verify my design decisions come from the PRD rather than being invented. The decision to test unexported functions in the same package is necessary for testing displayWidth. Table-driven tests are a Go convention but I need to check if they're actually specified in the PRD before including that as a design decision.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":46346,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73d1b541-a8ae-476d-94cc-76bc7ae96fed","uuid":"535924ea-2596-4565-99fc-b0705452d3b7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GpBgN95yVX72MKdh2uo6Qf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specs. This project derives specifications from C source, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: 4-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds utility implementations, `pkg/sys/` wraps platform syscalls, `pkg/format/` handles output formatting, `pkg/testutils/` provides the differential testing harness, and `magefiles/` exposes build targets.\n\n**Current State**: Source code exists on the working branch for `pkg/format/` (color.go, size.go, table.go), `pkg/sys/` (stat.go, stat_darwin.go, stat_linux.go, terminal.go), and `pkg/testutils/` (difftest.go). No test files exist for any of these packages (testfiles: 0 across all rel00.0 use cases). No `cmd/` code is on the working branch.\n\n**Current Release**: rel00.0 (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure). Four use cases:\n- uc001-magefiles: excluded from generation scope (magefiles/ path)\n- uc002-format: implementation exists, **tests missing**\n- uc003-sys: implementation exists, **tests missing**\n- uc004-testutils: implementation exists, **tests missing**\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nAll three pkg/ packages have implementations but zero tests. Per P8 deterministic ordering (alphabetical by primary file path when equal priority): `pkg/format/` → `pkg/sys/` → `pkg/testutils/`. The first task is tests for `pkg/format/` table alignment functions, anchored to prd003-format R1.1–R1.4.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Test pkg/format table alignment -- prd003-format R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml (R1 requirements for table alignment)\n      - pkg/format/table.go (implementation under test -- Columns, PadRight, PadLeft, displayWidth)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/table_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for Columns, PadRight, PadLeft, and displayWidth\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test Columns distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, and returns nil for empty input (prd003-format R1.1)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test PadRight and PadLeft pad strings to the specified display width with trailing or leading spaces, and return the string unchanged when it is already at or beyond the target width (prd003-format R1.2)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test displayWidth returns rune count, not byte count, for multi-byte UTF-8 strings (prd003-format R1.3)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test Columns uses row-major (across) order matching gls --format=across, with per-column widths computed from the longest entry in each column, not the global maximum (prd003-format R1.4)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Tests are in package format (not format_test) to access the unexported displayWidth function directly\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/format/ passes with all table_test.go tests green\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Columns returns nil for empty input and falls back to a single column when no multi-column layout fits within termWidth\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"PadRight and PadLeft are no-ops when string display width is already at or beyond the target width\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"displayWidth correctly counts runes for ASCII, multi-byte UTF-8, and empty strings\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Columns produces row-major order output -- entries fill left-to-right across rows before moving to the next row\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":46346,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73d1b541-a8ae-476d-94cc-76bc7ae96fed","uuid":"8c1c63b6-296d-4cb9-bee9-092cbc1f5115"}
